.TH BOOTSTASH-PROVISION-GOOGLE 8 "September 2026" "bootstash" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bootstash-provision-google \- write Google OIDC client keys into /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bootstash provision-google
[\fB\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-secrets\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-origin\fR \fIurl\fR]
[\fB\-project\fR \fIid\fR]
[\fB\-json\fR \fIpath\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints a four-step Google console recipe (project, branding,
Web application client, download JSON) with a recommended value
at each step, including the exact OIDC redirect URI
.RB ( $PUBLIC_ORIGIN/oidc/callback )
from the loaded config. It says whether that URI is
.B https
(PEMs found; BIND port stays) or
.B http
(no certs yet), and that finding certs does not move
.B BIND
to 443.
Then copies the downloaded Web application client JSON
.RB ( \-json
or a prompted path) to
.IR /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json
(mode
.BR 0640 ,
.BR root:bootstash ).
That file is the console
.B Download JSON
object
.RB ( web.client_id
/
.BR web.client_secret ).
Desktop
.RB ( installed )
JSON is rejected.
Run as root (or
.BR sudo )
so that write succeeds. It does not write TLS certs.
It does not edit
.IR /etc/default/bootstash .
.PP
It does
.B not
create the Google OAuth web client.
.B gcloud
has no supported command for that client type. Consent / branding is
once per GCP project. One web client per
.B PUBLIC_ORIGIN
(operator file,
.BR \-origin ,
or the
.B hostname \-f
default).
If
.B gcloud
is on
.BR PATH ,
the current project id is used for console links.
It does not create Unix users.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-config " path"
Operator config to read
.B PUBLIC_ORIGIN
from. Default:
.IR /etc/default/bootstash .
.TP
.BI \-secrets " path"
File to write. Default:
.IR /etc/bootstash/oidc-google.json .
.TP
.BI \-origin " url"
.B PUBLIC_ORIGIN
if it is not already in
.IR /etc/default/bootstash .
.TP
.BI \-project " id"
GCP project id for console URLs when
.B gcloud
has none.
.TP
.BI \-json " path"
Downloaded Web application client JSON to install.
Use
.B \-
to read stdin.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bootstash (8),
.BR bootstash (5)
